---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import Cite from '~/components/Cite.astro';
import EwaldLab from '~/islands/EwaldLab';

const assumptions = [
  { term: 'System', value: 'Ideal rock salt of point charges ±1 e, nearest-neighbour distance r₀ = 2.82 Å; conventional cubic cell (edge 2r₀, 4 Na⁺ + 4 Cl⁻), reference ion the Na⁺ at the cell origin.' },
  { term: 'Gaussians', value: 'Screening charge density ∝ exp(−α²r²); drawn as spheres of radius 1.5/α, which enclose most of each cloud.' },
  { term: 'Sums', value: 'Real space: all images with r < r_cut. Reciprocal space: k = 2πm/L with |m| ≤ m_max. Self term: −2α/√π. Lengths in r₀, potential in e/r₀ (multiply by k_e e/r₀ = 5.106 eV for NaCl).' },
  { term: 'Precision', value: 'erfc is evaluated to about 10⁻⁷ relative error, so differences below ~10⁻⁸ in M are not resolved.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ewald Lab"
  description="Physics Lab P6: split every Coulomb interaction into a short-ranged screened part and a smooth part, sum each where it converges fast, and watch the splitting parameter trade work between them without changing the answer."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P6</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Ewald Lab</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In <a href="/physics-lab/beyond-the-cutoff/#madelung">P1</a> the Coulomb sum of a crystal refused to converge over
        spheres. Ewald’s 1921 trick fixes it by splitting each charge in two. Here you control the split and both
        cutoffs, and see exactly where the work goes.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="live-calculation" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="idea-h">
      <h2 id="idea-h" class="h-section">The idea</h2>
      <p>
        Put a Gaussian cloud of opposite charge on every ion. Point charge plus cloud is neutral beyond a distance of
        about 1/<Math tex={String.raw`\sym{ewa}{\alpha}`} />, so its interactions die off fast and can be summed directly
        in real space. To keep the physics unchanged, add back the same Gaussians with the original sign. Those are smooth,
        so their sum converges fast in reciprocal space. Finally remove each ion’s interaction with its own cloud:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\frac{1}{r} = \underbrace{\frac{\operatorname{erfc}(\sym{ewa}{\alpha} r)}{r}}_{\text{screened}} + \underbrace{\frac{\operatorname{erf}(\sym{ewa}{\alpha} r)}{r}}_{\text{smooth}}, \qquad \varphi_i = \varphi^{\text{real}}_i + \varphi^{\text{recip}}_i - \frac{2\sym{ewa}{\alpha}}{\sqrt\pi}\,q_i`} />
      <p>
        Nothing in (1) depends on α except how the total is shared out. That is the experiment: move α and watch the
        green total stay flat while the parts swing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Ewald summation of the rock-salt Madelung sum" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={1} id="fig-ewald" framed={false}>
        <EwaldLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: the conventional cell with its neighbours; switch between the bare point charges, the screened charges
          (opposite-sign clouds) summed in real space, and the smooth clouds summed in reciprocal space. The teal sphere
          is the real-space cutoff. Right: the radial split at the current α; how the three contributions vary with α
          (their total is flat); and the error on a log scale against each cutoff.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={assumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Method: <Cite id="ewald1921" />. Computed in your browser; the total is checked against M = 1.747565 in the Atlas test suite.</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="trade-h">
      <h2 id="trade-h" class="h-section">The trade-off α controls</h2>
      <p>
        Wide Gaussians (small α) make the reciprocal part converge after a handful of <b>k</b> vectors but leave long
        real-space tails; narrow Gaussians (large α) do the opposite. Choosing α to balance the two costs makes the
        classic Ewald sum scale as about <i>N</i><sup>3/2</sup> in the number of atoms. Mesh methods put the smooth part on
        a grid and use fast Fourier transforms, bringing the cost down to <i>N</i> log <i>N</i>
        <Cite id="darden1993pme" />.
      </p>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Why this matters for MLIPs">
        Long-range MLIPs predict charges, or charge-like latent variables, from local environments and pass them to
        exactly this machinery, for example latent Ewald summation <Cite id="cheng2025les" />. The local network decides
        <em>what</em> the charges are; Ewald decides how they interact at every distance, periodic images included.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-page {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem) clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  .head .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 4.4vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }
  .lede {
    font: 1.1875rem/1.6 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink-muted);
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .lede a,
  .block a {
    color: var(--atlas-arch);
  }
  .head-badges {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font: 1.0625rem/1.7 var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--atlas-ink);
  }
  .block p {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }
  .fig-wrap {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
